Shader preparation and submission:
  Source: Conversation agreement: Prepare(lambda) edits persistent shader inputs; Use() invokes Submit().
  Goal: Stage input changes without GPU side effects, then publish the complete current state before drawing or dispatching.
  Scope: Graphics and compute shaders, shared shader bases, resource bindings, and all application/engine callers.
  Excludes: Same-frame allocation caching, cross-frame upload reuse, and a new benchmark project.

  ✔ Establish the lifecycle contract and inventory consumers.
    - Contract: Rendering.ShaderPreparation.md; migration sites: Rendering.ShaderPreparation.Inventory.md.
    - Source review, second planning pass and independent completion audit passed; documentation only, no runtime changes.
    - Define a strongly typed Prepare(lambda) API that exposes each concrete shader's parameters.
    - Preserve defaults and all unassigned values/references across Prepare calls, Use calls, and program reloads.
      Preparation is synchronous; multiple calls accumulate changes. Resource ownership remains with existing owners.
    - Define protected Submit() and enforce an implementation for every concrete shader, including shared-base submission.
      Resolve abstract versus virtual enforcement explicitly; do not silently permit omitted submission.
    - Specify nested preparation, preparation exceptions, and Use during preparation; never submit an incomplete preparation.
      Do not introduce transactional rollback without a separate requirement.
    - Inventory immediate setter uploads/binds, raw uniform setters, texture/image/sampler/buffer inputs,
      compile-time options, generated bindings, compute wrappers, and engine-owned draw callbacks.
    - Identify permitted assignments outside Prepare and how development checks enforce the agreed boundary.
    - Completion: Document the API and enumerate migration sites and justified engine adapter exceptions.

  ☐ Implement the shared lifecycle and migrate upsample as the reference consumer.
    - Setters stage CPU values or resource references only; preserve existing CpuUniformBuffer packing/change tracking.
    - Use ensures the requested generation is ready, activates it, updates cached program state, then calls Submit.
      Submit uploads each owned parameter block once and binds the current resources through the installed contract.
    - Propagate submission failure through Use/TryUse so callers cannot draw with incomplete required inputs;
      retain contract handling for optional/optimized-out resources and define activation cleanup on failure.
    - Handle repeated Use, same-program nested UseScope, scope restoration, and engine/interface dispatch explicitly.
      Restoring a previous program must also restore the resources required by its prepared state.
    - Stage LumOnUpsampleShaderProgram inputs first in LumOnRenderer, then Use and draw; retain previously set values.
    - Completion: Focused subagent-run tests prove partial preparation, no setter uploads/binds, and one publication per block per Use.

  ☐ Migrate all shaders and callers to the contract.
    - Move binding/upload behavior into concrete Submit implementations, composing shared frame/world-probe inputs.
    - Convert graphics and compute call sites to prepare inputs before activation/submission and draw/dispatch.
    - Give engine-driven liquid and other adapters a verified pre-draw submission boundary before removing setter publication.
    - Preserve prepared state across recompilation while resolving bindings against the current program generation.
      Keep ring lifetime rules: clean CPU state alone cannot suppress a required upload or binding.
    - Remove obsolete immediate-submit helpers and unused UBO batch scopes; update remaining batch semantics and comments.
    - Completion: Inventory reconciled; each remaining immediate-binding entry point is an explicit low-level operation or documented adapter.

  ☐ Validate and document the completed contract.
    - Run builds and focused CPU/GPU tests through subagents: retained/default values, repeated partial preparation,
      preparation/submission failure, nested scopes/restoration, reloads, missing required/optional resources,
      frame rollover, changed snapshots remaining valid for earlier draws, and persistent/fallback UBO uploads.
    - Verify upload counts for several changed assignments followed by one Use; distinguish count reduction from frame-time performance.
    - Update shader-authoring guidance with the reference usage and resource lifetime responsibilities.
    - Perform a second review and independent audit against this checklist before marking complete.
    - Record headless evidence separately from user-run rendering checks; do not launch the game for acceptance.
